Re-review round 3, all four findings fixed.
(1) DESTRUCTIVE recreate: compose now never recreates what exists. HTTP start is `compose up -d --no-recreate SERVICE`; stdio deps `compose up -d --no-recreate DEPS`, the server `compose run --rm -T --no-deps`. Rule held: the hub may start a dep that does not run, never stops/recreates/restarts one that exists. A compose that rejects a flag fails the start with "... the hub needs Docker Compose 2 or later (docker compose version); podman-compose is not supported" (podman-compose also lacks `config --format json`). Measured live on compose v5.5.1/rootless Podman: default policy "diverged" recreates a running dep whose env-interpolated config differs; --no-recreate keeps its id.
(2) dependency waits out of the handshake: the stdio prepare polls `compose ps -a --format json DEPS` every 0.5s until service_healthy deps are healthy and service_completed_successfully deps exited 0 (non-zero exit / exited / unhealthy / no healthcheck fail the start with a message naming the dep). Not `up --wait` (fails on a one-shot dep: "container ... exited (0)", exit 1; and a stop can't cut it short), not `compose wait` (doesn't see exited containers). No timeout, as compose has none; a stop ends the wait at once. One-shot deps re-run on every start, as compose does.
(3) docs reworded with the exact sentence (README Compose section + "What the hub stops and removes", Compose.pm POD, spec As-built).
(4) fake models the recreate policy (config hash per container; never/force/diverged; restarts keep the id), depends_on conditions (healthcheck x-healthy-after/x-health, oneshot kind x-exit/x-runtime), strict flags. New docker.t subtests: 'compose: a dependency that exists is never recreated, stopped or restarted' (HTTP + stdio; fails on the old tree: db and cache ids change) and 'compose: what the service waits for is ready before the handshake' (fails on the old tree: tool times out after 5s, messages differ, no version hint).
